Coolpg Report
[Author: Alejandro]
1. Executive Summary

During the assessment, a full attack chain was identified that led from an unauthenticated SQL Injection vulnerability to full root compromise of the system. The attack involved:

UNION-based SQL Injection

Credential extraction

SSH access

Privilege escalation via misconfigured sudo

The overall impact resulted in complete system compromise.

2. Reconnaissance

An aggressive full TCP port scan was performed against all 65,535 ports to ensure complete service discovery.

Open Ports Identified:

22/tcp – SSH

80/tcp – HTTP (nginx)

The web application was analyzed manually. A footer revealed a fully qualified domain name (FQDN), which was added to /etc/hosts for proper resolution.

3. Enumeration

Directory enumeration was performed against the identified virtual host using Gobuster.

Three significant endpoints were discovered:

Login 

Search 

panel

The search functionality contained a comment in the page source indicating that UNION inputs were not sanitized.

Initial testing confirmed injectable behavior:

Normal input:

test

Result:

username not found

Injection test:

' UNION SELECT 'test'--

The response reflected controlled output, confirming a UNION-based SQL Injection vulnerability.

4. Exploitation
Database Identification

The following payload was used to identify the database engine:

' UNION SELECT version()--

The output confirmed PostgreSQL.

Column Enumeration

The number of columns was determined by adjusting the UNION SELECT statement until the query executed successfully.

NULL values were used to match column count safely:

' UNION SELECT NULL, NULL, NULL--
Table Enumeration

Relevant tables were identified, including:

users

secrets

The users table contained administrative credentials, but logging in redirected back to the same panel.

The secrets table contained sensitive information, including:

SSH credentials

Username

This provided initial system access.

5. Initial Access

SSH login was successfully performed using the extracted credentials.

Upon login:

User flag was located.

Home directory contents were analyzed.

Log files were inspected.

6. Privilege Escalation

Privilege enumeration revealed a misconfigured sudo entry:

NOPASSWD: /usr/local/bin/runlogs-find.sh

The script could be executed as root without password authentication.

By leveraging this configuration, root privileges were obtained.

7. Impact

The attack chain demonstrates the following security failures:

SQL Injection due to lack of prepared statements.

Sensitive SSH credentials stored in the database.

Privilege escalation via overly permissive sudo configuration.

Successful exploitation resulted in:

Full database access

Unauthorized SSH access

Root-level system compromise

This represents total server compromise.

8. Remediation Recommendations
Application Layer

Implement parameterized queries / prepared statements.

Apply strict input validation.

Remove sensitive data from application-accessible tables.

Credential Management

Avoid storing SSH credentials in databases.

Enforce least privilege access.

System Hardening

Remove unnecessary NOPASSWD sudo entries.

Restrict script execution permissions.

Audit sudo configurations regularly.
